      "raw_response": "ANSWER: SUPPORTED. The abstract suggests that perceived age, which is closely linked to an individual'CTAG1,2,3,4,5.\n\n### Explanation:\n\nThe abstract reports a study where perceived age was found to be significantly associated with survival, even after adjustments for chronological age, sex, and rearing environment. It also found that the likelihood of the older-looking twin dying first increased with the increasing perceived age difference within the pair. Additionally, perceived age correlated with physical and cognitive functioning and leucocyte telomere length, which are important age-related phenotypes. The study supports the claim that cellular aging is closely linked to an older appearance, as perceived age, which reflects physical and cognitive functioning, and biological markers of aging, correlates with survival.\n\n\n### Citation:\n\nVilkaitis, L., Pedersen, N. C., Walgemo, A., et al. (2008). Perceived age-a robust biomarker of ageing that predicts survival among those aged 70 and older. Aging Cell, 7(5), 685\u2013692. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1361-6358.2008.10.1111/j.1364-6981.2008.00081.x",
